00:00:04.309 --> 00:00:06.470 align:start position:0%
hey everyone i'm john coogan and welcome  to my guide to startup fundraising  last week i talked about closing a deal  and went into some details about the  safe  today i want to talk about what happens  when you close your round and start to  plan for the next one if you're  following my advice from the last video  then you're probably raising with  standard post money safes that have a  valuation cap and no discount  but fundraising is a messy process and  even though you might have a few angel  investors already committed on those  terms a larger investor could come along  and want to do a priced round so what  happens then  as we've mentioned before your goal as a  founder is to build a great business  and that means making your customers  happy growing your customer base  significantly every month  and finding a large market you can't  focus on any of that if you're tied up  in fundraising negotiations for months  at a time  that's why so much work has been done by  the startup community to simplify  fundraising  raising a priced round means you will  actually be issuing shares to the  investor  as opposed to signing an agreement to  give them shares in the future  because shares are actually changing  hands a price round is usually more  complicated than using safes  but that doesn't mean it's worth walking  away from a great investor just because  they want to do a priced round  let's go through some of the pros and  the cons of both the safe and the price  round  first is speed if you're using a  standard safe like the one on y  combinator's documents page  you can execute it in a single day using  an e-signature service like docusign  these documents have been used thousands  of times and there's nothing to  negotiate except for the valuation cap  with a priced round the closing process  usually takes at least a few weeks  which could be distracting if you're in  a particularly hectic period of growth  the second is cost since you don't need  a lawyer to review it  the safe essentially costs nothing to  execute priced rounds not only require  you to have your lawyer review documents  and manage a closing process  often you will be required to pay for  the legal costs of the lead investor  this can be as low as 5k  or as high as 60k a good rule of thumb  though  is to multiply whatever the investor is  asking for in legal reimbursement by  three since you will likely need to do  more legal work on your end to close the  deal  the third is terms price rounds have  very different expectations about what  terms are included by default  it's common for priced rounds to have  founder vesting requirements  board seats block rights and lots of  other items that will need to be  negotiated line by line  to be fair all of these items can be  included in safe side letters or  excluded from priced round documents but  the default state here matters  it's easier to ask an investor not to  add a side letter to a safe  than to ask that investor to remove an  item from a priced round term sheet  lastly there is sequencing because safes  can be executed individually  you can close the first investor very  early and avoid a situation where an  investor is soft committed  but waiting for you to get a lead  investor before they sign documents or  wire any money  additionally it's not uncommon for the  announcement of a fundraising round  to attract more investors and it's  harder to capitalize on those when  you've already closed a priced round  with safes you can always just issue one  more safe to get that investor in  this is a double-edged sword though on  the one hand it can be great to roll  your fundraising from 100k to 1 million  as you gain heat on your round  but on the other hand having safes at  different valuation caps can make  tracking your total dilution very  difficult  being overwhelmed by complicated safes  is so common that it even has a nickname  the waterfall to put it simply the  waterfall is a situation where the  founder loses control of the round  by issuing dozens of safes at increasing  valuations  no single safe in this case causes  massive dilution but taking together  they can add up to 30 or more percent of  the company  it's essentially a death by a thousand  cuts  in 2017 pascal levinson and andrew crown  published an article  on the waterfall effect in techcrunch  and while it does a good job of warning  entrepreneurs about the risk of early  stage dilution  i think it wrongly targets the safe  since the waterfall effect can also  occur  when founders use convertible notes fear  of the waterfall alone  isn't enough of a reason in my mind to  abandon safes entirely  but if you do want to do a priced round  here's my advice  try and use standard documents from  siriseed.com whenever possible  they're simpler than what you would be  using if you did a series a  and it's much cheaper and quicker to  execute second  make sure you understand what provisions  are included in the docs that you're  signing  there are generally two types of  provisions in a financing document  financial provisions and control  provisions financial provisions govern a  few things  mainly who owns the financial interest  in the company whether or not they have  the right to buy more shares in the  future  and what protections they have if the  company sells or goes bankrupt  control provisions are all about who  runs the board of directors  who can hire and fire the executives and  who makes the final decisions on big  company issues  if you look around the technology  industry you will notice that many  companies have special provisions where  the founders control the company  even though they don't own 51 percent or  more of the financial interest in the  business anymore  whether you care more about financial  provisions or control provisions  will depend on your personal life goals  but if you really want to understand  deal structures and the associated  trade-offs  i recommend reading venture deals by  brad feld and jason mendelsohn which  i've linked below  regardless of the exact investment  vehicle you use once you close the deal  it's your job to get back to building  but things will be different in a few  key ways  you'll need to send regular updates to  your investors and hold board meetings  even if the founders are the only board  members  since investors expect further growth  you need to start solidifying your key  performance indicators or kpis  and start thinking about what milestones  you want to hit before your next round  when it comes to investor updates the  key to success is focusing on a single  consistent metric  like active users or revenue and  committing to a cadence  with everything else going on at your  startup on a daily basis it can be  really easy to let these updates slip  so create a schedule that you can stick  to consistently  aside from your kpis you'll also want to  include information about important  product milestones  new customer wins key hires and any asks  you have for the investors  investor updates are a great way to get  your investors to add value and help you  make connections that they wouldn't have  thought of otherwise  running an effective board meeting could  be the subject of an entire video  but in short you should figure out what  topics you want to discuss  then build a board deck circulate it  beforehand to everyone who's going to  attend  and then make sure to present the  decisions that were made at the meeting  to the rest of the team so everyone  stays aligned  in terms of your series a milestone you  will need to forecast your primary kpi  out roughly 12 to 18 months to figure  out what growth rate you need  to hit that goal if you're tracking  monthly revenue your investors should be  able to help you understand what monthly  revenue numbers they are typically  seeing at series a for your sector  as well as how fast that number should  be growing once you have that milestone  in mind  it's your responsibility to deploy the  funds you raise judiciously  raising a series a will always be  significantly harder than raising a seed  round  so you need to plan for profitability  never assume that you will be able to  raise more money  no matter how easy the last round was  avoid letting your expenses spike and  focus on letting growth justify hiring  it's common for investors to recommend  hiring fast and in some cases that is  the best strategy  but you need to make sure that the new  hires will justify the added costs  investors often overemphasize hiring  quickly  this is because as investors they're  used to deploying capital to solve  problems  and encouraging overspending to a small  degree  will lead to bigger future rounds where  they can buy a larger share of the  company  just make sure that you have a solid  financial plan and can get to  profitability if need be  the market can shift at the drop of a  hat and you need to be able to react  quickly to stay alive  even though i've now published nearly a  full hour of content on fundraising  it's important to try and keep it as  simple as possible  things can get complicated quickly and  that will drain the energy you need to  build a great business  the easiest way to keep things simple is  to use the fundraising best practices  outlined here  and try and push back on any  complications that are introduced by  investors  ultimately fundraising is a game of give  and take understanding the trade-offs  between different investors  documents and terms will give you the  ability to close your round quickly and  focus on building a great business  if you enjoyed this video or this series  please consider subscribing to the  channel for more content  and if you have any questions just leave  me a comment below i'm definitely  reading them  thanks for watching
 

